{
  "scene_id": "scene05_limits_of_manual_logic",
  "video_title": "What Is Machine Learning?",
  "scene_title": "The Limits of Manual Logic",
  "order": 5,
  "duration_mode": "audio_driven",
  "voice": {
    "language": "en-IN",
    "speaker": "priya",
    "model": "bulbul:v3"
  },
  "narration": [
    {
      "id": "s5_01",
      "text": "You might ask: if traditional programming works so well, why do we need machine learning at all?"
    },
    {
      "id": "s5_02",
      "text": "The answer is that many real-world problems are too messy for hand-written rules."
    },
    {
      "id": "s5_03",
      "text": "Take spam detection. At first, you might write a simple rule: if the email contains the word Winner, mark it as spam."
    },
    {
      "id": "s5_04",
      "text": "But then spammers change the spelling. Then they use images. Then they use symbols. Then they hide the message in other ways."
    },
    {
      "id": "s5_05",
      "text": "Very quickly, the rule set becomes complicated and weak."
    },
    {
      "id": "s5_06",
      "text": "The same problem appears in many other areas."
    },
    {
      "id": "s5_07",
      "text": "How do we write rules to detect a pedestrian in a self-driving car system? How do we detect a small tumor in a noisy medical scan? How do we recommend a movie or song based on a person's behavior?"
    },
    {
      "id": "s5_08",
      "text": "These tasks involve many variables at once. They are hard to describe with fixed rules, but often easier to learn from data."
    },
    {
      "id": "s5_09",
      "text": "That is why machine learning exists. It helps us work with patterns that are too complex for manual rule-writing."
    }
  ],
